WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.200
When Iron Man premiered in 2008, no one could
have possibly anticipated its influence.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.490
Now, what had been predicted to be a mildly
successful B-tier film at best has the distinction

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.049
of anchoring one of the most successful franchises
in film history.

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:16.860
So what's with the curious absence of a fourth
Iron Man movie?

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.090
Let's dig through the gossip and find out
why we never got to see Iron Man 4.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.660
Pricey playboy

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.230
Robert Downey, Jr.'s value to the Marvel Cinematic
Universe can't be overstated, since he's been

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.090
the charismatic center of the Avengers initiative.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.309
This on-screen value, however, comes with
a real-world price tag.

00:00:33.309 --> 00:00:36.880
As Tony Stark, Downey hasn't just been one
of the most valuable players in the Marvel

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.870
stable, he's become one of the most valuable
actors in the entire world.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.620
Even modest estimates have his earning capacity
at hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.190
It would be no surprise if the cost-benefit
analysis of another Iron Man, from Marvel's

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.909
side, shows less and less of a reward.

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:55.249
Fortunately, the end of Downey's involvement
wouldn't have to spell the end for a big-screen

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:56.249
Iron Man.

00:00:56.249 --> 00:01:00.539
His version — beloved as it is — is just
a part of Iron Man's legacy, rather than a

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:04.150
role that ends with his departure, as others
have been known to step into his rocket-shoes.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.680
"Basically I am here to announce that we are
building Iron man.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.190
I'm gonna blast off in a second."

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.640
Too many heroes

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.469
If you're a fan of Marvel movies, you've likely
heard about their strategy of releasing films

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:19.469
in "phases."

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:23.780
Phase 1 was the original "Avengers assemble"
arc, introducing our main heroes.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:28.520
Phase 2, leading up to Age of Ultron, broadened
that world, taking us into space and introducing

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.939
new heroes like Ant-Man.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:34.979
Now we're in Phase 3, set to introduce a slew
of new heroes, culminating with the long-awaited

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:39.369
battle against Thanos in 2018's Avengers:
Infinity War.

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:43.450
Meanwhile, Captain America has already had
his third adventure, and Thor will also see

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.750
his third movie before Phase 4 begins.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.049
Add Black Panther and Captain Marvel's upcoming
solo movies, and it's hard to see how another

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:52.530
Iron Man could easily fit into the mix.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.259
Short on villains

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:56.969
Bad guys have never really been the strong
point of the MCU.

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:01.010
They're all fairly one-note, and most of their
master plans boil down to blowing up buildings

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.740
for 20 minutes before getting their butts
handed to them by the heroes.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.689
We're sort of running out of interesting and
impressive villains that are specific to Iron

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:08.689
Man.

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:12.019
There are still interesting directions they
can go, but throwing a giant, angry, hovering

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:13.989
head into the MCU seems unlikely…

00:02:13.989 --> 00:02:16.280
"That is a very big head."

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.040
...and most of Tony Stark's other enemies
are just other dudes in super-suits.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.849
And there's no way that we'll ever see them
throw a giant dragon like Fin Fang Foom into

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:25.270
the MCU.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:25.830
Or would they?

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.730
Short on good storylines

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.390
Iron Man's most famous storylines have mostly
already made it to the big screen, for better

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.390
or for worse.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.690
"Armor Wars" was loosely adapted for the premise
of Iron Man 2.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.990
"Demon in a Bottle," where Tony Stark confronts
his alcoholism, has been addressed throughout

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:45.990
the series.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.350
"You know the question I get asked most often
is, 'Tony how do you go to the bathroom in

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.230
the suit?'

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.540
Just like that."

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.870
"Extremis" was a pretty big part of Iron Man
3.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.290
At this point, Marvel might be kind of tapped
out.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.299
Would it be a good idea to go more obscure?

00:03:01.299 --> 00:03:05.840
Can moviegoing audiences deal with Tony Stark
being mind-controlled by Kang the Conqueror

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.730
and beating up his teenage self?

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.340
Could it be Marvel would rather not push its
luck?

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.849
Find out in the next episode of.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:14.500
An unnecessary risk

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.720
The Iron Man trilogy is, in critics' eyes,
a three-for-three success story, which is

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:19.989
no small task.

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:24.769
Ask the makers of the Spider-Man, X-Men, and
Batman franchises how well their third installments

00:03:24.769 --> 00:03:25.269
went.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.409
Not even The Godfather was as consistent as
Iron Man.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:35.170
Executives may be hesitant to risk that perfect
record, because they stand to lose more than

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.459
they stand to gain.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:40.920
It's easy to make bad superhero movies with
terrible plot contrivances…

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.610
"Save Martha."

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.680
... but the conclusion of Iron Man 3 felt
like a smart final statement for the character

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.670
as a solo hero… and the insane action sequence
in which Stark summons an army of robot-controlled

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.159
Iron Man suits to save him from an unstable,
explosive villain, seems kind of impossible

00:03:58.159 --> 00:03:59.159
to top.

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:00.530
We don't need Iron Man 4, not really.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.630
The next best thing is right around the corner.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:03.630
Thanks for watching!

